---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/cybersecurity/foundational/security-foundations-the-cia-triad">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Cybersecurity, and it assumes nothing — no networking, no
    programming, no prior encounter with a hacker. If you can follow a household budget, you
    can follow everything here, because the core of security is a way of accounting rather
    than a bag of tools.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name and explain the three properties — confidentiality, integrity, availability — that every security decision protects, trades off or sacrifices;</li>
    <li>use the risk vocabulary precisely: asset, threat, vulnerability, safeguard — and the arithmetic that decides whether a defence is worth its cost;</li>
    <li>describe the recurring attack doors (phishing, malware, exploitation, denial of service, insiders) and who walks through them;</li>
    <li>explain why defences are layered, and calculate what layering actually buys;</li>
    <li>recognise the profession's shared frameworks — NICE, the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, ISO 27001 — and say what each one is for.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit to carry through the whole subject: whenever you meet a security tool, ask which
    of the three properties it protects, which attack it slows, and what it costs. Tools age
    in years; the questions never age at all.
  </p>

  <h2>The stranger in the coffee shop</h2>
  <p>
    A student in a coffee shop opens her phone and connects to what looks like the shop's
    free Wi-Fi. It is not. Sitting in the corner, someone has set up a look-alike hotspot
    with the same name, and now everything she sends — including her bank login — passes
    through the stranger's laptop. Nothing was "hacked" in the movie sense. One property of
    one conversation was violated: the conversation was not private. Hold that scene; it is
    this entire subject in miniature.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="bet before you read on — what actually happened" widget="predict" config={ciaEvilTwinPredict} />
  <p>
    It also explains why security looks different from other engineering. Most engineering
    fights nature: gravity, friction, weather. Cybersecurity fights an opponent —
    intelligent, adaptive, and actively trying to defeat you. A bridge never studies its
    designer's habits and waits for the unguarded afternoon; an attacker does. So the
    discipline's answers are distinctive too: layered defences that expect to fail, spending
    justified by the cost of the incidents prevented, and a shared vocabulary precise enough
    that a hospital's engineers, auditors and board can reason about the same threat without
    talking past each other.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="nature vs opponent — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={ciaOpponentCompare} />
  <p>
    The vocabulary rests on two pillars. The first is the <strong>CIA triad</strong> —
    confidentiality, integrity, availability — the three properties every security decision
    protects, trades off or sacrifices. (The acronym has nothing to do with the intelligence
    agency; the letters simply list the properties.) The second is <strong>risk</strong>:
    the admission that nothing can be perfectly defended, so protection must be prioritised
    by how likely an attack is and what it would cost. Every framework you will ever meet —
    NIST's, ISO's, a company's internal policy — is an elaboration of those two ideas.
  </p>

  <h2>The triad, property by property</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Confidentiality</strong> is the property that information is seen only by the
    people meant to see it. Universities live inside it daily: privacy law requires that
    grade records be restricted to staff with authorised access — the "need to know"
    principle — so nobody else may learn anything about their contents. Confidentiality is
    enforced by <em>encryption</em> (scrambling data so that only holders of the key can
    read it), by access control (keeping the curious out), and by classification schemes
    (so everyone agrees what counts as secret). Its failures are the headlines: leaked
    customer databases, intercepted messages, a laptop of unencrypted patient records left
    in a taxi.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each enforcement tool with its job" widget="match" config={ciaConfidentialityMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Integrity</strong> is the property that data truly represents what it is meant
    to represent — no tampering, no silent corruption, no unauthorised edits. The word is
    borrowed from personal integrity: saying what you mean, consistently. A hired hacker
    changing a student's grade breaks integrity deliberately; a power surge corrupting a
    file, or an authorised clerk deleting the wrong record, breaks it just as thoroughly by
    accident. It is enforced by cryptographic hashes and digital signatures (any alteration
    becomes detectable), by input validation (so attackers cannot rewrite what a system
    accepts), and by audit trails (so every change carries a name and a time).
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — what integrity actually means" widget="judge" config={ciaIntegrityJudge} />
  <p>
    Note the asymmetry between the first two properties. A confidentiality breach leaks
    information. An integrity breach can <em>steer the physical world</em> — a manipulated
    sensor reading, an altered payment — while looking completely normal, which is why
    integrity failures are often the quieter and more dangerous kind.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="leak or tamper? file each failure" widget="sort-buckets" config={ciaBreachSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>Availability</strong> is the property that information and systems can be used
    by anyone authorised, within an appropriate time frame. The frame depends on the job: a
    stock trader needs prices within seconds, a salesperson is content with yesterday's
    figures in a morning report, and an online retailer's servers must answer day and night.
    Availability is engineered with redundancy, capacity headroom and tested backups, and
    attacked with denial-of-service floods, plain old outages — and ransomware.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Ransomware</em> — malicious software that encrypts a victim's files and demands
    payment for the key — repays study because it attacks two properties at once:
    availability (your files are held hostage) and integrity (even restored data may not be
    trustworthy if the attackers spent weeks inside the network). A classic delivery: an
    employee receives what looks like the CEO's quarterly-earnings attachment, opens it, and
    the encryption of the company's data begins quietly in the background.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="the routine-looking attachment — walk it" widget="branch" config={ciaRansomBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three words name everything security protects. Confidentiality
    controls who can see data, integrity guards what data says, availability guarantees the
    system answers when needed. Every breach you have ever read about is a failure of at
    least one of them — but no property can be pushed to its maximum, as the panel below
    makes visible.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the triad and its trade-offs" widget="equationStep" config={ciaCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the panel and watch each property arrive
    with its enforcement tools and its characteristic failures — then the two steps after
    them, where the real lesson lives. Perfect confidentiality is an air-gapped computer
    with no network, no USB ports and no users — and no usefulness. Perfect availability is
    a system with no locks. The triad is not a checklist to maximise; it is a set of dials
    to set deliberately, and the method for choosing the settings is risk.
  </p>

  <h2>Risk: security's accounting</h2>
  <p>
    Because resources are finite and perfection is impossible, security decisions are made
    with an accountant's vocabulary. An <em>asset</em> is anything worth protecting: a
    customer database, a design file, the ability to take payments. A <em>threat</em> is who
    or what could harm it: a criminal gang, a careless employee, a flood. A
    <em>vulnerability</em> is your own weakness a threat could use: an unpatched server, a
    missing lock, a server room below the water line. A <em>safeguard</em> is the control
    you add to shrink the danger. A house makes the relationships vivid: the house is the
    asset, the burglar is the threat, the open window is the vulnerability, the lock is the
    safeguard — and no lock ends the story, because a determined burglar brings tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    The profession's classic arithmetic makes the conversation concrete. Suppose a
    server-room flood would cost 200,000 dollars in a single loss, and floods of that size
    are expected roughly once a decade — a rate of 0.1 per year. The expected cost is then
    20,000 dollars per year. A safeguard costing 5,000 dollars a year that meaningfully
    reduces that risk is justified; one costing 50,000 dollars is not. Probabilities for
    rare events are famously uncertain, and that is fine: the point of the arithmetic is not
    precision but forcing the right questions — what is this control <em>for</em>, and is
    that what it costs?
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="call the annualised bill" widget="estimate" config={ciaFloodEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the goal of security is to prevent all attacks." It is
  not — it is to make successful attacks rare, brief and cheap. Organisations that assume
  prevention will hold perfectly are the ones devastated when it does not; organisations
  that invest in detection and response turn a catastrophe into a bad Tuesday. "Assume
  breach" is not defeatism; it is engineering for the failure mode you cannot eliminate.</p>

  <h2>Who attacks, and through which doors</h2>
  <p>
    Attacks arrive through a small set of recurring doors, and the first is the human.
    <em>Social engineering</em> attacks people rather than machines — most of all through
    <em>phishing</em>: messages crafted to trick you into clicking, revealing a password or
    opening an attachment. Industry breach surveys consistently find the human element
    involved in a large majority of incidents, which tells you where the cheapest door
    usually is.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Malware</em> is software doing harm: viruses, ransomware, spyware, remote-access
    tools. <em>Exploitation</em> uses software weaknesses — injection flaws, memory bugs,
    misconfigurations — often years after they were documented and a patch was published.
    <em>Denial of service</em> attacks availability directly, drowning a service in traffic
    until genuine users cannot get through; the distributed version (DDoS) coordinates many
    machines at once, so the flood cannot be stopped by cutting off one source. And the
    <em>insider</em> walks in the front door with valid credentials — maliciously, or far
    more often by being fooled.
  </p>
  <p>
    Who is behind it? Threat actors sort into a few kinds by motivation. <em>Amateurs</em> —
    the traditional name is "script kiddies" — run tools downloaded from the internet with
    little skill, sometimes out of curiosity, sometimes to show off; the outcomes can still
    be catastrophic. <em>Hacktivists</em> attack to make a political or social point,
    leaking documents or crippling websites. <em>Cyber criminals</em> work for money:
    stolen card data, account takeovers, ransomware. <em>Nation-states</em> spy and
    sabotage; the Stuxnet worm, widely reported to be state-engineered, travelled on USB
    drives into the computers controlling nuclear centrifuges and physically damaged them —
    malware reaching into the material world.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real intrusions chain several doors rather than using one, which is why defenders
    describe them as a sequence — the "kill chain". A phishing email lands; a user clicks;
    malware executes; credentials are stolen; the attacker moves sideways toward the data;
    the copying-out begins. Each link is a defensive opportunity, and the chain framing
    turns a vague fear into an engineering checklist: which links can we break, and at what
    cost? It also exposes a truth newcomers resist — most successful attacks are not
    brilliant; they are patient uses of known weaknesses, unpatched systems and reused
    passwords.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="the intrusion as a sequence — break the chain" widget="order" config={ciaKillChainOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "attackers are mostly genius hackers, and my small
  accounts are beneath their notice." In reality the bulk of cybercrime is automated and
  indiscriminate: bots scan the entire internet continuously, and credential-stuffing tools
  replay passwords stolen from one breach against thousands of other sites. Contrast the two
  pictures: a genius picks targets; the algorithm cannot tell how small you are. "Too small
  to matter" is the most dangerous sentence in the field.</p>
  <p>
    Attackers have budgets, and the defender's strategy falls straight out of theirs. Bulk
    crime — phishing campaigns, credential stuffing, commodity ransomware — is cheap,
    automated and impatient, so it abandons targets that resist and moves on; against it,
    inexpensive controls applied everywhere (patching, multi-factor authentication, mail
    filtering) fail nearly all the attempts at nearly no cost. Targeted intrusion —
    espionage, sophisticated extortion — is expensive and patient: the attacker researches,
    waits out your defences, and accepts weeks of work. Prevention alone cannot be the whole
    answer to that class, so the strategy splits in two: make cheap attacks fail cheaply
    everywhere, and assume the expensive ones will occasionally succeed. That is why
    detection, response and rehearsed recovery are the second half of the defence, not
    consolation prizes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> attacks arrive through a handful of doors, pulled open by a
    handful of actor types, chained into sequences that ordinary preparation can break. Now
    for the defender's structural answer — and its arithmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>Defence in depth, quantified</h2>
  <p>
    Since no single control is reliable, security's structural answer is layering —
    <strong>defence in depth</strong>, borrowed from the castle that depends not on one wall
    but on moat, outer wall, inner wall and garrison. Mail filtering and phishing training
    come first; endpoint protection next; then least-privilege access and multi-factor
    authentication; then network segmentation and monitoring behind them. The reason
    layering works is arithmetic: an attacker must clear every layer in sequence, so the
    survival rates <em>multiply</em>. Suppose phishing reaches and fools one user in ten
    (10 percent), malware evades the endpoint in a quarter of cases (25 percent), and
    stolen credentials succeed at the access layer 15 percent of the time. Passing all three
    means 0.10 × 0.25 × 0.15 = 0.375 percent — roughly one adversary in 267 clears the
    whole stack.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="layer three controls and multiply the survivors" widget="sliderLab" config={layersCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the sliders and keep your eye on the readout — the
    number that matters is the product, never any single layer. Two lessons hide in the
    multiplication. Improving your <em>weakest</em> layer pays more than perfecting your
    strongest: raise the 25 percent layer and the product drops sharply; polish an already
    strong layer and the gain is modest. And each new <em>independent</em> layer multiplies
    the survivors down again — the defender's compounding interest. The caveats are equally
    real: three products sharing one signature database are one layer wearing three logos,
    and if a single vendor outage disables two "separate" controls at once, the layers were
    an illusion. Depth is a property of an architecture, not a shopping list.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "security is a product you can buy" — one good firewall
  or antivirus subscription and the job is done. The layered arithmetic says otherwise: a
  control protects only the layer it sits at, an unpatched application or a phishable
  employee routes straight around any single box, and attackers chain weaknesses rather than
  challenge defences head-on. Tools are layers; security is the architecture — plus the
  patching, the multi-factor authentication and the rehearsed response that make the layers
  hold.</p>

  <h2>Security vocabulary and frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The profession coordinates itself through shared reference frames, and knowing the map
    is half of joining it. The <em>NICE Workforce Framework</em> (published by NIST, the US
    National Institute of Standards and Technology) names the work itself — defending,
    analysing, operating, overseeing, building — and the job roles under each, which is why
    job postings read like its categories. The <em>NIST Cybersecurity Framework</em>
    organises any programme into six functions — govern, identify, protect, detect, respond,
    recover — a full lifecycle from boardroom policy to post-incident restoration; version
    2.0, published in 2024, added "govern" as an explicit function. <em>ISO 27001</em>
    certifies that an organisation manages security risk systematically — not that it is
    unhackable, but that the management around the technology is disciplined.
  </p>
  <p>
    Underneath the frameworks run shared artefacts. The <em>CVE catalogue</em> gives every
    publicly known vulnerability a name and number, so the whole world can discuss one flaw
    unambiguously; the <em>CVSS</em> scale scores its severity from 0 to 10. And underneath
    those sit principles old enough to be laws: least privilege (every person and program
    gets the minimum access needed, and no more), separation of duties (no single person can
    both make and approve a critical change), defence in depth, and "fail securely" (when a
    system breaks, it should break closed rather than open).
  </p>
  <p>
    Two habits make the frameworks usable rather than decorative. First, map your own world
    onto them concretely: pick one asset — say, the customer database — and walk the six
    framework functions against it. The gaps name themselves: no identified owner, no
    detection tuned to queries against it, no rehearsed response if it is encrypted on a
    Sunday. Second, use the shared vocabulary when describing incidents: "a phishing
    delivery (threat) exploited the absence of multi-factor authentication (vulnerability)
    to breach the confidentiality of payroll data (asset, CIA property)" is one sentence a
    board, an auditor and an engineer can all act on. Vagueness is expensive; the frameworks
    exist to make it inexcusable.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A hospital's patient-monitoring system is briefly altered so it displays normal readings while a real problem exists. Which CIA property failed, and why is that failure especially dangerous?</li>
    <li>A warehouse flood would cost 100,000 dollars and is expected about once every 20 years. What is the annualised expected loss, and is a 3,000-dollar-a-year safeguard that prevents most of the damage justified?</li>
    <li>Name the four kinds of threat actor and one motivation of each.</li>
    <li>Three independent controls each block an attack 90 percent of the time. Roughly what fraction of attacks pass all three?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Integrity — the data no longer represents reality, and
    integrity failures are the dangerous kind because they can steer the physical world
    while looking completely normal: clinicians would act on fiction. (2) 100,000 ÷ 20 =
    5,000 dollars per year; a 3,000-dollar safeguard is justified, because it costs less
    than the expected loss it removes. (3) Amateurs (curiosity or showing off), hacktivists
    (politics), cyber criminals (money), nation-states (espionage and sabotage). (4)
    0.1 × 0.1 × 0.1 = 0.1 percent — about one in a thousand; that is the compounding power
    of independent layers.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The CIA triad — confidentiality, integrity, availability — names the three properties every security decision protects, trades off or sacrifices.</li>
    <li>Security's accounting is risk: asset, threat, vulnerability, safeguard; expected annual loss = cost of one incident × incidents per year, and a safeguard is justified when it costs less than the loss it removes.</li>
    <li>Attacks come through a few recurring doors — social engineering (mostly phishing), malware, exploitation, denial of service, insiders — and real intrusions chain several of them.</li>
    <li>Threat actors sort by motivation: amateurs, hacktivists, criminals, nation-states; bulk attacks are automated and indiscriminate, targeted ones patient and expensive.</li>
    <li>Defence in depth works by multiplication: independent layers compound, so improving the weakest layer pays most.</li>
    <li>NICE names the jobs, the NIST Cybersecurity Framework names the lifecycle, ISO 27001 certifies the management; CVE and CVSS give vulnerabilities names and severities.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson descends into the machinery the triad protects: how networks and
    operating systems actually carry and store data, because every attack door studied here
    opens onto that machinery. After that comes cryptography in practice — the mathematics
    that enforces confidentiality and integrity — and then the attack techniques themselves.
    The triad and the risk arithmetic you built here survive all of those lessons unchanged:
    they are the questions, and everything after is new ways of answering them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The triad is a model, and models leak. Practitioners add properties it underweights:
    <em>authenticity</em> (is this message really from its claimed sender?), accountability
    (can actions be attributed to a person?) and <em>non-repudiation</em> (can the sender
    later deny having sent it?) — digital signatures provide these in ways plain
    confidentiality never can. Availability has its own deep literature and its own
    economics: the cost of an outage usually grows faster than its length, which is why
    "five nines" — 99.999 percent uptime, about five minutes of downtime a year — is a
    genuine engineering target and not a marketing flourish.
  </p>
  <p>
    The research frontier is where the interesting discomfort lives. <em>Security
    economics</em> — a field Ross Anderson helped found — shows that misaligned incentives,
    the party who can fix a vulnerability rarely being the party who bears the loss, explain
    chronic insecurity better than any technical deficit. <em>Zero-trust architecture</em>
    abandons the trusted internal network as a concept: verify every request, every time —
    which quietly redesigns everything. Quantum computing threatens today's public-key
    cryptography on a schedule nobody can pin down, and "harvest now, decrypt later" makes
    the threat current for long-lived secrets, hence the migration to post-quantum
    algorithms already under way. Machine learning cuts both ways, automating phishing at
    scale while automating detection at scale. Through all of it, the CIA triad and the risk
    arithmetic survive intact — which is the final lesson: in a field where every tool ages
    in dog years, the durable curriculum is the way of thinking.
  </p>

